## Installation

Add the dependency to your Gradle build:

```kotlin
dependencies {
    implementation("com.ekino.oss:karbone:<version>")
}
```

Snapshots (built from `main`) are published to the Central Portal snapshot repository:

```kotlin
repositories {
    mavenCentral()
    maven { url = uri("https://central.sonatype.com/repository/maven-snapshots/") }
}
```

## Creating a client

Against Carbone Cloud, with an API key:

```kotlin
val karbone = Karbone.cloud(apiKey = "YOUR_API_KEY")
```

Against a self-hosted `carbone-ee` (see [On-premise](on-premise.qd) for running one):

```kotlin
val karbone = Karbone.onPremise("http://localhost:4000", token = "YOUR_JWT")
```

`token` is optional on-premise: pass nothing (or `null`) when the container runs with authentication disabled.

Both factories accept a trailing configuration block for timeouts, retries, JSON and more — see [Configuration](configuration.qd).

## Rendering a document

Every Karbone call is a suspending function that needs a `Raise<KarboneError>` in scope. The simplest way to get one is Arrow's
`either { ... }`, which turns the whole block into an `Either<KarboneError, T>`:

```kotlin
import arrow.core.raise.either
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.Karbone
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.PdfVersion
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.RenderData
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.TemplateSource
import java.nio.file.Path
import kotlinx.serialization.json.buildJsonObject
import kotlinx.serialization.json.put

suspend fun renderInvoice() {
  val karbone = Karbone.onPremise("http://localhost:4000", token = "YOUR_JWT")

  val template = TemplateSource.file(Path.of("invoice.docx"))
  val data =
    RenderData.of(
      buildJsonObject {
        put("number", "F-2026-001")
        put("total", 1234.5)
      }
    )

  val result =
    either {
      karbone.renders.render(template, data) {
        pdf { version = PdfVersion.PDF_A_3 }
        lang = "fr-fr"
      }
    }

  result.fold(
    { error -> println("Render failed: ${error.describe()}") },
    { document ->
      println("Got ${document.fileName} (${document.content.size} bytes)")
      document.writeTo(Path.of("invoice.pdf"))
    },
  )
}
```

`template` is hashed locally (SHA-256) and rendered directly; Karbone only uploads it if Carbone does not already know that
hash. `render` returns a `RenderedDocument` with `content: ByteArray`, `fileName` and `contentType`; use `inputStream()` or
`writeTo(path)` to consume it. See [Rendering](rendering.qd) for the full set of options.

## Java / blocking callers

Java has no coroutines and no `Raise`. `Karbone.blocking()` (Kotlin: any `KarboneClient.blocking()`, Java: `KarboneBlocking.of(client)`)
returns a synchronous facade where every call runs to completion on the calling thread and throws `KarboneException` on failure.
Because it works over the `KarboneClient` interface, the same facade wraps `FakeKarbone` in tests:

```java
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.Karbone;
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.KarboneException;
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.blocking.KarboneBlocking;
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.RenderData;
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.RenderOptions;
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.RenderedDocument;
import com.ekino.oss.karbone.model.TemplateSource;

KarboneBlocking karbone = Karbone.onPremise("http://localhost:4000", "YOUR_JWT").blocking();

try {
    RenderedDocument document =
        karbone.getRenders().render(
            TemplateSource.bytes(templateBytes, "invoice.docx"),
            RenderData.raw("{\"number\":\"F-2026-001\"}"),
            RenderOptions.None);
    // use document.getContent(), document.getFileName()...
} catch (KarboneException e) {
    // e.getError() is the KarboneError value
    System.err.println(e.getMessage());
}
```
